---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import { WDS_URL } from '../consts';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who makes the Warbox Game Companions, and why they are free."
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Site" title="About" icon="shield" lead="Who makes these companions, and why." />

  <div class="wrap section">
    <Note tone="draft">
      Draft copy. The personal parts — who Norbi is, how he got into these games, which ones he
      keeps coming back to — are his to write; this is scaffolding so the page exists and reads
      sensibly in the meantime.
    </Note>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        I play Wargame Design Studio games, I make spreadsheets to help me read them, and I put the
        spreadsheets here so other people can use them too. That is the whole site.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the companions exist</h2>
      <p>
        WDS games hold an enormous amount of detail, and most of it is genuinely useful once you can
        see it. The problem is never that the information is missing — it is that you are reading it
        through a small window, a few rows at a time, while trying to hold a plan in your head.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I started pulling it out into spreadsheets: one scenario per row, orders of battle side by
        side, victory points where you can compare them at a glance. They were for me first. It
        turned out they were worth sharing.
      </p>

      <h2>What is here, and what is coming</h2>
      <p>
        I own around thirty WDS titles and I work through them steadily. New companions appear as
        they are finished; games that are on the list but not done yet are marked as in progress, so
        you can see what is coming rather than wonder.
      </p>

      <h2>Not official</h2>
      <p>
        This is a fan site. It is not affiliated with Wargame Design Studio, not endorsed by them,
        and nothing here is official material. The games themselves are theirs, and they are worth
        buying — <a href={WDS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">wargameds.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Free, and staying that way</h2>
      <p>
        No account, no email, no donation button. Download what is useful to you. If you find a
        mistake, <a href="/contact/">tell me</a> — that is payment enough.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
